INT. LIGHTHOUSE LAMP ROOM TEA SHOP - DUSK

The lamp room of a decommissioned lighthouse, glazed on all sides, the great lens long ago swapped for six mismatched tables. Salt has frosted the windows from outside. Inside, steam from a copper urn beads on the brass rail. Every cup in the room is chipped in the same place, as if by one clumsy hand.

ODALYS VANCE, forties, a insurance assessor in a rain-spotted camel coat, sits with her back to the sea. Her notebook is closed. Her pen is not.

BRAM OKONKWO, sixties, the shop's owner, a broad man with flour-white forearms and a limp he pretends is a stroll, sets a teapot between them without being asked.

HESTER PELL, eighties, sits at the farthest table in a knitted hat, spooning sugar into a cup that is already full. She has not looked up since Odalys arrived.

ODALYS
The policy was taken out eleven days before the fire.

BRAM
Eleven days is a good number. Lucky, in some places.

ODALYS
The fire started in the cellar. The cellar had no wiring. No stove. Nothing that burns by itself.

BRAM
Old buildings do surprise us.

ODALYS
Your lease says the cellar was sealed in 1994. Someone unsealed it. Someone with a key to a door that, on paper, does not exist.

BRAM
I have a great many keys. Lighthouses are sentimental about keys.

ODALYS
Mr. Okonkwo, I am not accusing you.

BRAM
That is the most frightening sentence in your language.

HESTER
(not looking up)
He did not light it.

ODALYS
Mrs. Pell. I did not know you were listening.

HESTER
I have listened to this room for fifty years. It is the only thing I do properly.

BRAM
Hester, please.

HESTER
Your father kept the lamp, Bram. Mine kept the logbook. You know what a logbook is for.

BRAM
Not now.

ODALYS
What is it for, Mrs. Pell?

HESTER
For the nights nobody wants remembered.

BRAM
That book burned.

HESTER
That book is in my handbag.

ODALYS
Then the fire was not an accident, and it was not for the insurance.

BRAM
Stop. Both of you. Stop talking.

HESTER
He wrote a name on page forty. A boy. Seventeen. The wrong rocks, the right storm. Your father let the lamp go dark that night, Bram, to save oil.

BRAM
He was ordered to.

HESTER
He was paid to.

ODALYS
And the cellar?

HESTER
The cellar is where the boy was kept until the tide went out.

The urn hisses. Bram's hand closes on the teapot handle and the pot shatters against the brass rail, tea running black across the floorboards toward Hester's shoes.

BRAM
Nobody was meant to ever open that door!
